public static int minPerfectSquareSum(int n) {
    int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        dp[i] = i;
        for (int j = 1; j * j <= i; j++) {
            dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i - j * j] + 1);
        }
    }
    return dp[n];
}
```
This method uses dynamic programming to find the minimum number of perfect square numbers that sum up to n. It uses a 1D array dp to store the minimum number of perfect square numbers for each number from 1 to n. The base case is dp[0] = 0, since no perfect square numbers are needed to sum up to 0.

For each number i from 1 to n, we iterate from 1 to the square root of i and check if j * j is a perfect square number. If it is, we update dp[i] to be the minimum of dp[i] and dp[i - j * j] + 1. The + 1 is because we need to add one more perfect square number to the sum.

Finally, we return dp[n], which is the minimum number of perfect square numbers that sum up to n.

